## 1. What Git Is

- Git is a version control system.
- It lets me keep track of changes I make to my code.
- Instead of having files like:
    project_final.py
    project_final2.py
    project_final_REAL.py
    project_final_REAL_v2.py

  I can use Git to save different versions of my project properly.

- Git runs locally on my computer.
- I do not need the internet to use Git.
- Git records changes to files inside a Git repository.
- Git is mainly used through the terminal.

- Git is useful because I can:
    - Track changes
    - Undo mistakes
    - See what I changed
    - Create different versions of a project
    - Experiment without breaking my main project
    - Work with other developers
    - Keep a history of my project

---

## 2. What GitHub Is

- GitHub is a website/platform built around Git.
- Git and GitHub are not the same thing.

- Git:
    - Runs on my computer
    - Tracks changes
    - Manages versions
    - Works without the internet

- GitHub:
    - Stores Git repositories online
    - Lets me back up projects
    - Lets other people see my code
    - Makes collaboration easier
    - Provides tools for issues, pull requests, actions, etc.

- A simple way to remember it:

    Git = version control

    GitHub = online platform for Git repositories

- I can use Git without GitHub.
- I can also use GitHub with other Git hosting services such as GitLab or Bitbucket.

